WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.840
So now that we've talked about all this,

00:01.840 --> 00:03.680
let's take a look at the code that we've been building.

00:03.680 --> 00:06.080
So if you go ahead and look at our types right now,

00:08.000 --> 00:11.880
you might notice that both the dish and the restaurant type

00:11.880 --> 00:14.400
share this property called status.

00:14.400 --> 00:17.160
And more importantly, status is just a string.

00:17.160 --> 00:19.520
When a lot of us know that when it comes to letting users

00:19.520 --> 00:21.880
choose something, it's best to have some sort of options

00:21.880 --> 00:23.720
that they consistently use.

00:23.720 --> 00:25.320
And so if we go ahead and take a look back

00:25.320 --> 00:28.040
at our restaurant's page and take a look at it.

00:28.040 --> 00:29.720
So we can see here that, okay, status is,

00:29.720 --> 00:31.600
we have want to try and recommend it.

00:31.600 --> 00:33.280
Do not recommend, okay.

00:33.280 --> 00:35.920
And then dishes page, similarly, there's want to try,

00:35.920 --> 00:37.800
recommend it, do not recommend.

00:37.800 --> 00:40.720
Okay, so now this is where we're seeing a use case

00:40.720 --> 00:42.560
for having shared types and more importantly,

00:42.560 --> 00:45.240
we want something that's a little bit more specific

00:45.240 --> 00:47.920
than just generic string type that we have.

00:47.920 --> 00:51.280
So we're gonna learn now is learn how to define those things.

00:51.280 --> 00:54.120
So the way we're gonna do this is I'm just gonna call it

00:54.120 --> 00:55.640
a type of status.

00:55.640 --> 00:57.160
Let's just gonna call it.

00:57.160 --> 00:59.640
And what we're gonna learn about here is the union operator

00:59.640 --> 01:02.160
which basically allows you to define specific values

01:02.160 --> 01:04.520
for the type rather than just a generic,

01:04.520 --> 01:06.480
like data primitive or whatever.

01:06.480 --> 01:09.720
So in this case, we had, I think it was want to try.

01:09.720 --> 01:12.840
I think it was recommend and then I think it was

01:12.840 --> 01:14.720
do not recommend.

01:14.720 --> 01:16.060
Okay.

01:16.060 --> 01:18.680
And so then what we can do here is rather than just

01:18.680 --> 01:21.160
putting the generic string, we can actually say,

01:21.160 --> 01:23.040
oh, this is gonna be a type status.

01:24.120 --> 01:26.040
And what that allows you to do now is that when you hover

01:26.040 --> 01:27.760
over this, you can see that it actually tells you

01:27.760 --> 01:29.800
the exact ones that it allows.

01:31.080 --> 01:33.440
Now if we go back over now to our page,

01:33.440 --> 01:35.640
you'll notice something kind of funny.

01:35.640 --> 01:37.680
Status is yelling at us.

01:37.680 --> 01:39.920
And the reason for this is because it's saying,

01:39.920 --> 01:43.320
hey, I don't recognize this value.

01:43.320 --> 01:44.800
In other words, when it says something's overloaded,

01:44.800 --> 01:46.480
usually doesn't recognize the value.

01:46.480 --> 01:48.240
And it's saying, what is recommended?

01:48.240 --> 01:50.240
And so I'm like, well, I thought I'd define it

01:50.240 --> 01:53.640
as recommended, but let's go ahead and check the logbook.

01:53.640 --> 01:56.280
Nope, we didn't, we called it recommend.

01:56.280 --> 01:59.120
And so it's little things like this that can make a huge

01:59.120 --> 02:00.960
difference for helping to start putting really

02:00.960 --> 02:03.560
some shape to your application when it comes to like, okay,

02:03.560 --> 02:05.880
this is the information architecture we're looking for.

02:05.880 --> 02:08.280
These are the options we want to limit people to.

02:08.280 --> 02:10.480
And so in this case, I think recommended is better

02:10.480 --> 02:12.480
than recommend, because that was a type one my end.

02:12.480 --> 02:15.040
And so now if I save it, you'll see that that just

02:15.040 --> 02:17.600
automatically transposes over and same deal.

02:17.600 --> 02:20.600
Now on the Dishes page, if I go ahead and I'm like, oh,

02:20.600 --> 02:24.480
I think Dishes should also get like a delicious status.

02:24.480 --> 02:28.200
You know, like that's fine that I did that,

02:28.200 --> 02:29.520
but then at ways I get maybe it's not,

02:29.520 --> 02:31.240
because it's not actually matching, right?

02:31.240 --> 02:32.960
We want them to, like at this point,

02:32.960 --> 02:35.080
we want them to share just a little bit easier.

02:35.080 --> 02:39.720
So this is, and so you see something that's simple

02:39.720 --> 02:41.480
is just creating that custom type.

02:41.480 --> 02:42.680
And then dropping it between the two,

02:42.680 --> 02:45.080
we see already, there's a ton of benefit.

02:45.080 --> 02:47.160
Especially when these types are reused multiple times

02:47.160 --> 02:49.200
throughout your codebase, now you're getting those alerts

02:49.200 --> 02:51.880
that like, hey, this property you're adding

02:51.880 --> 02:54.440
doesn't exist or has the wrong types now,

02:54.440 --> 02:56.920
and we can get even more specific.

02:56.920 --> 03:00.840
Okay, so we've now created our own custom type.

03:00.840 --> 03:04.240
And so I want you all to practice creating your own custom type.

03:04.240 --> 03:08.480
And what we're gonna be doing is I want you to create a diet.

03:08.480 --> 03:09.800
So actually, I forgot.

03:09.800 --> 03:12.680
I have this constant file here to give some guidance,

03:12.680 --> 03:14.160
as far as what this might be.

03:14.160 --> 03:16.880
So basically, you know, when it comes to eating food,

03:16.880 --> 03:18.360
dietary restrictions are a thing.

03:18.360 --> 03:20.320
So I just created four options to keep it simple.

03:20.320 --> 03:23.200
There's vegetarian, pescatarian, keto, and then there's blank.

03:23.200 --> 03:24.520
Cause some of them just, you know,

03:24.520 --> 03:26.800
if it's like everything, then it doesn't matter.

03:26.800 --> 03:29.760
And so I'd like you to create your own custom type.

03:29.760 --> 03:31.360
Don't worry about referring to the constants,

03:31.360 --> 03:33.400
let's just reference the values,

03:33.400 --> 03:35.360
because we'll be taking care of that in the next one.

